% mod_functor_call.glp - `mod` in CALL/FUNCTOR form `mod(...)`.
% The lexer emits ATOM `mod` when `mod` is immediately followed by `(`, else keyword MOD.
% So `mod(` heads a normal functor application in head / goal / term positions, while a
% space-separated `mod` stays the infix arithmetic operator. Exercises the mod-functor
% tokenization (feature 069 T016): before the fix the ANTLR lexer made `mod` always MOD and
% rejected `mod(...)` in head/goal (where atomApp requires ATOM); now both front-ends agree.

% `mod` as a user PROCEDURE name — `mod(` in HEAD atomApp position; the body uses the
% space-separated infix `mod` (keyword MOD) in the SAME clause:
exported procedure mod(Number?, Number?, Number).
mod(X, Y, Z?) :- Z := X? mod Y?.

% `mod(...)` as an arithmetic term in a `:=` RHS (term-context functor application →
% StructTerm("mod",[X?,Y?]), the same node infix `X? mod Y?` produces):
exported procedure use_mod(Number?, Number?, Number).
use_mod(X, Y, Z?) :- Z := mod(X?, Y?).
